= Long-term outcomes of cruciate ligament injury: attempting a matched cohort analysis of New Zealand linked register data
#smallcaps[Published]\ 14 April 2023

#smallcaps[Citation]\ #eval("Pryymachenko Y,Wilson R, Abbott JH. Long-term outcomes of cruciate ligament injury: attempting a matched cohort analysis of New Zealand linked register data. OSF, 14 April 2023. doi:~#link(\"https://doi.org/10.17605/osf.io/zfuyg\")[10.17605/osf.io/zfuyg]", mode: "markup")

=== Abstract
*Background*
Little is known on the long-term effects of cruciate ligament
(CL) injuries. The present study aimed to investigate the effect of CL injuries
on long-term health and socioeconomic outcomes in New Zealand using national-level
observed data.

*Method*
We used a comprehensive linked database to identify
the majority of CL injuries in New Zealand, and to find matched control cases
based on a large set of covariates. Regression analyses were then performed to
identify the long-term effects of CL injury.

*Results*
13#sym.space.nobreak.narrow;176
CL injuries were identified during 2008#sym.dash.en;2009. CL injuries were associated
with significantly increased healthcare utilization (number of hospitalizations,
days spent in hospital, emergency department visits, medications dispensed) and
costs within 10 years post-injury, as well as increased likelihood of having a
total knee replacement or a subsequent CL injury. However, we also found that
having a CL injury was also associated with improved socioeconomic outcomes and
reduced mortality, which is surprising and may indicate bias due to unobserved
differences in physical activity levels between the treatment and control groups.

*Conclusions*
We have concerns that the results of this study may be biased
by unmeasured confounding. Further research, accounting for physical activity
levels between individuals with and without a CL injury, is needed to establish
the true effect of CL injury on long-term outcomes.

